  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nPrecipitation continues across portions of southern Montana and \nnorthern Wyoming this afternoon. The upper trough over the Four \nCorners and a surface low over the Dakotas will continue eastward \nthrough this evening. This will pull the precipitation with it, \nending it from west to east. Mountain snowfall for north-west \nfaces of the Absaroka/Beartooths, Pryors, and Bighorns will \ncontinue into the afternoon as well.\n\nBreezy conditions will continue into the afternoon as well with a\ntight pressure gradient over eastern Montana and northern \nWyoming. Gusts of 25 to 45 mph are occurring and a few gusts into \nthe low 50s mph are possible, mainly in far eastern Montana along \nthe Dakotas border and in southern Sheridan County. Winds will \ndecrease this evening as the system departs the region.\n\nA Winter Weather Advisory remains in effect until 8 PM MST this \nevening for the Bighorn Mountains, with additional snowfall up to \n6 inches forecast and the windy conditions resulting in \nsignificant reductions to visibility from blowing snow. Locally \ngreater amounts are possible at the higher elevations. The bulk of\nadditional snow accumulations will fall above 7,000 to 7,500 \nfeet. If you have plans to travel through the Bighorns on Highway \n14 or have recreation plans in the high country, prepare for very \ndifficult conditions.\n\nFor additional precipitation amounts, eastern Montana looks to be\nfavored with 0.25\" to over 0.75\" of rainfall forecast through \nthis afternoon. For portions of northern Wyoming downsloping has \nkept the area drier than what models previously depicted. Sheridan\nAirport has yet to pickup any rainfall yet today, though this \nshould change as precipitation moves south-east with the \naforementioned system movement. For locations around and north- \nwest of Billings expect lighter accumulations the rest of today.\n\nPatchy fog may develop for much of the forecast area east of \nLivingston tonight into Tuesday morning. Use caution during the \nmorning commute as reductions to visibility could impact travel.\n\nThe rest of the week looks warmer and drier with ridging. High \ntemperatures are forecast in the upper 40s and 50sF Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday. Above normal temperatures are in store to end \nthe work week, with highs in the 50s to low 60sF. A few showers \nmay move through the mountains on Friday with an upper wave, \notherwise the chance for precipitation is very low this week.\n\nMatos",
